## Behavior → Technique → Telemetry

Reference: [MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise matrix](https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/enterprise/) (v19)

| Attacker behavior | Technique | Tactic(s) | Where it shows up (telemetry) | What to look for |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Exploits a bug or misconfiguration in an Internet-facing app to get a first foothold | [T1190 Exploit Public-Facing Application](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1190/) | [Initial Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0001) | Web server and WAF logs, process-creation events on the server (Sysmon 1 / auditd), egress flow logs or PCAP | Crafted requests, then a 4xx/5xx spike, then the web server process spawning a shell or curl/wget, then an outbound connection to a new host.<br>MITRE detection: [DET0080](https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0080) |
| Guesses or cracks passwords to get into accounts | [T1110 Brute Force](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/) | [Credential Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0006) | Windows Security log (4625 failures, 4624 successes), Linux auth log (sshd), IdP sign-in logs, VPN logs | Many failed logons followed by a success.<br>Guessing: many failures on one account.<br>Spraying: few failures across many accounts.<br>Stuffing: many accounts, mostly unique passwords, often distributed IPs.<br>MITRE detection: [DET0463](https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0463) |
| Logs in with real (stolen, guessed or default) credentials, so it looks like a normal user | [T1078 Valid Accounts](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078/) | [Initial Access](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0001), [Persistence](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0003), [Privilege Escalation](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0004), [Stealth](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0005) | IdP sign-in logs, Windows 4624 (logon type), VPN/RDP logs, cloud audit logs, Linux auth and sudo logs | The login is legitimate, so check context: unusual source, time, logon type or geography; a service account logging in interactively; impossible travel; repeated MFA prompts or failures.<br>MITRE detection: [DET0560](https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0560) |
| Hides command-and-control traffic inside normal protocols (HTTP, DNS, mail) | [T1071 Application Layer Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/) | [Command and Control](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0011) | NetFlow / PCAP, proxy logs, DNS logs, EDR network-connection events (Sysmon 3) | Regular beacon intervals (with jitter), unbalanced flows, an odd process speaking HTTP/DNS, irregular ports, long or high-entropy DNS names, TXT-heavy lookups.<br>MITRE detection: [DET0444](https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0444) |
| Floods a target's network link to make it unreachable | [T1498 Network Denial of Service](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1498/) | [Impact](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0040) | NetFlow / sFlow, interface counters, CDN or edge analytics, PCAP | Link saturation, packet-rate spikes, traffic from amplification ports. Source side: a host sending huge outbound volume or running flooding tools like hping3 / nping.<br>MITRE detection: [DET0518](https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0518) |

